{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789517177,"nonce":"J1ndALtY2jQQ2jBYCDrJEWt6tvw=","title":"Python: a local asserted to be an instance is that instance","message":"richards' idiom in every fn: `d = r` followed by `assert isinstance(d, DeviceTaskRec)`, with r a base-class or dynamic parameter. The local is typed as the asserted class when the assert follows its only binding directly; the binding then does the check, raising AssertionError as the assert would, and casts, and the assert itself is spent. Fields read through the local are direct reads instead of dynamic attribute lookups.","files":null}]}